  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nSurface low remains analyzed over the Central Plains with surface \nhigh moving towards Ontario. This continues allowing for drier air \nto win out over most of the state, except parts of the \nsouthwest, resulting in little overall precipitation. Light \nrain over parts of the southwest should increase in coverage \nlater this afternoon and especially overnight tonight. After \nwhich, rain chances should gradually diminish through Sunday \nnight. A quarter to half inch of additional rain is likely over \nthe far southwest, but probabilities rapidly drop off further \nnorth and east. Near the Missouri River, probabilities for even\na tenth of an inch drop down to around 50 percent, and rapidly \nless further north and east of the river.\n\nDespite the lack of precipitation across most of the state, \nlow-level stratus will prevail overall tonight through Sunday. \nThough by late Sunday afternoon and through the night, skies \nshould begin to clear from north to south. This thick sky cover \nwill likely moderate temperatures with forecast lows remaining \nwarmer than the NBM produces, and highs potentially cooler than \nforecast... more so for this afternoon than Sunday. \n\nDry conditions are then expected to prevail Monday through \nTuesday before a shortwave potentially brings another round of \nshowers, possibly with a few thunderstorms, through the area \nWednesday and Wednesday night. Seasonable temperatures are \nexpected to start the next workweek with highs initially in the \n60s to low 70s. A gradual warming trend is then favored to \nbring highs into the 70s with some low 80s degree readings in\nwestern ND by the end of the week.",
